#
# <template file>:<absolute destination>[:<cleanup glob>]
#
# configd writes every one of these as root:wheel with the default umask, so the
# `synapseidssensor fixperms` action (and the rc.d start_precmd) tighten them
# immediately afterwards:
#   sensor.conf          -> root:wheel 0640                 (the instance index, no secrets)
#   instances/NAME.conf  -> root:wheel 0640                 (per-sensor flags, no secrets)
#   sensor.token         -> _synapseids:_synapseids 0400    (the bearer token, nothing else)
#   sensor-ca.pem        -> root:wheel 0444                 (public trust material)
#   sensor-cert.pem      -> root:wheel 0444                 (public certificate)
#   sensor-key.pem       -> _synapseids:_synapseids 0400    (THE TLS PRIVATE KEY)
#
# The two 0400 entries are the reason fixperms exists: configd's umask would
# otherwise leave a freshly rendered secret world readable until somebody
# noticed.  Every caller of `template reload OPNsense/SynapseIDSSensor` runs
# fixperms straight afterwards.
#
# THE SECOND LINE IS THE ONE TO READ CAREFULLY (issue #124).
#
# The bracketed tag is configd's repeating-target syntax: `%` enumerates the
# repeating <instance> nodes and the trailing `.name` supplies the value that
# goes into the file name, so ONE template produces ONE FILE PER SENSOR
# INSTANCE, and each render receives TARGET_FILTERS telling it which instance it
# is.  This is core behaviour (Template.__find_filters in
# /usr/local/opnsense/service/modules/template.py), not a plugin invention; the
# same mechanism is what lets a template emit one file per interface.
#
# The third field is the cleanup glob for `configctl template cleanup`, which
# would otherwise only remove the literal target - a name containing an
# unresolved `[...]` that exists on no disk. It is NOT what keeps stale instance
# files from accumulating: the `fixperms` action removes any instances/*.conf
# that no longer matches a configured instance, and the rc.d script only ever
# acts on the names listed in synapseids_sensor_profiles, so a leftover file is
# inert even before it is deleted.
sensor.conf:/usr/local/etc/synapseids/sensor.conf
sensor-instance.conf:/usr/local/etc/synapseids/instances/[OPNsense.SynapseIDSSensor.instances.instance.%.name].conf:/usr/local/etc/synapseids/instances/*.conf
sensor.token:/usr/local/etc/synapseids/sensor.token
sensor-ca.pem:/usr/local/etc/synapseids/sensor-ca.pem
sensor-cert.pem:/usr/local/etc/synapseids/sensor-cert.pem
sensor-key.pem:/usr/local/etc/synapseids/sensor-key.pem
